Date: Fri, 31 Dec 93 02:19:14 GMT
From: LI10000@phx.cam.ac.uk
Subject: Flow - the psychology of happiness

I'd like to suggest a  recent (1992) book by Mihaly
Csiskszentmihalyi [a Chicago psychologist]: "Flow: the
Psychology of Happiness" [Harper & Row].  Its organising theme
is that happiness requires not so much that our environment is
pleasant but that we succeed in imposing order on the naturally
chaotic state of consciousness.  Flow experiences (often achieved
through activities such as chess, rock climbing and athletics) are
enjoyable because they stretch you to the limit of your ability in
achieving self-chosen goals.  They are extropian because they are
the result of a temporary success in our continuous struggle to
vest our life with meaning - we emerge from them as more
complex, updating our ambitions in line with our newly improved
skills

Although he is not explicitly libertarian he explores many threads
that should appeal.  The discussion of how people in acutely
entropian situations - concentration camps etc - maintain sanity
and inner peace is a bit reminiscent of the Heinlein novel (I forget
the plot and title - something to do with Job) where the hero
manages to cope despite being continually flung between parallel
universes at the whim of a playful god.  Many of the lessons that I
drew allowed me to profitably read Browne's Freedom in an
Unfree World once again - right now I find it difficult to pin down
exactly what the common ground is, but this may become clearer
later.  [Incidentally, how have people found the experience of
putting some of the techniques he (Harry Browne) suggests into
practice (I'd be interested to hear here or by email) - what
problems, needed modifications etc?]

Modern Austrian economists marvel at the manner in which the
market process encourages complexity by rewarding coordinating
arbitrage as well as socially useful innovation; there is at least a
superficial parallel between these Hayekian themes of
coordination and discovery and Csikszentmihalyi's of integration
and differentiation.  He calls for an evolutionary faith (value
system) that is not only enlightened by an appreciation of the
"origins of instinctual drives, social controls, cultural expressions
- all the elements that contribute to the formation of
consciousness" but affirms complexification as an ought.  This
seems startlingly similar to the suggestion of a similar yardstick
by Tyler Cowen (once an Austrian fellow-traveller)  in "Towards
a non-Paretian Welfare Standard" published in Don Lavoie's
"Economics and Hermeneutics".



