Date: Fri, 31 Dec 93 02:22:11 GMT
From: LI10000@phx.cam.ac.uk
Subject: Feasibility of g.e.?

Brief challenge for those who would reengineer their genetic
software without hesitation.  Viktor Vanberg (paraphrasing
Hayek) might have been talking about biological evolution
rather than the market when he said that "It is a process that
facilitates adaptation in a world in which our knowledge and the
problems we face change in ways that can never be fully
anticipated, a process that, for these very reasons, by necessity
always leads into the unknown".  Obviously, the attributes we
admire and would like future generations to exhibit are not
identical with those that lead to evolutionary success but there is
enough of an overlap for many of us to consider that the results
are not likely to be a disaster.  Given our genuine ignorance
both of the future, and perhaps of all the non-obvious
consequences of making a particular change, would it be
dangerously close to Hayek's fatal conceit to leapfrog evolution
and take a long jump approach to engineering?

I've drawn quite a lot on a paper by Vanberg that might itself be
of interest.  He discusses competitive political orders and the
implications for European federalism in the light of their
advantages.  The title is "Constiutionally Constrained and
Safeguarded Competition in Markets and Politics With
Reference to a European Constitution" - I've only got a draft so
it was probably published last year or will come out fairly soon.



