J oh N writes:
>I hope that selling the rights to the book won't mean that you are
>restricted in any way from releasing the database/software
>version.  Wouldn't the latter effectively be a superset of the book?

I'm not a lawyer, but I watch one on TV...
The facts are not copyrighted, only the specific book.  Although the 
database will agree on scientific points with the book, it will not 
duplicate any specific text in the book.  Only the ideas are the same.  I 
also have documentation that the database existed before the book, and can 
show that all the data was gathered without using the book.  The book 
merely draws on the same public domain sources of data as the database.

I also made clear to the publishers that I was not selling them any rights 
to my research, except for the book.  I also made it clear that I might 
want to sell nutritional software or databases.
